# English meanings written for this site (original wording, not copied from any translation).
CH1 = [
 "Sanjaya, on the sacred field of Kurukshetra, my sons and the sons of Pandu gathered, eager to fight. What did they do?",
 "Seeing the Pandava army drawn up for battle, King Duryodhana went to his teacher Drona and spoke.",
 "Look, teacher, at this mighty army of the sons of Pandu, arrayed by your own gifted student, the son of Drupada.",
 "Here are heroes and great archers, equal to Bhima and Arjuna in battle: Yuyudhana, Virata and the great chariot-warrior Drupada;",
 "Dhrishtaketu, Chekitana and the valiant king of Kashi; Purujit, Kuntibhoja and Shaibya, the best of men;",
 "the bold Yudhamanyu, the brave Uttamaujas, the son of Subhadra and the sons of Draupadi, all of them great chariot-warriors.",
 "Now hear, best of the twice-born, about the leading warriors on our side. I name the commanders of my army for you.",
 "Yourself, Bhishma, Karna and Kripa, victorious in battle; Ashvatthama, Vikarna and the son of Somadatta.",
 "And many other heroes ready to give their lives for me, armed with many kinds of weapons, all skilled in war.",
 "Our army, guarded by Bhishma, is beyond measure; theirs, guarded by Bhima, is limited. (Readings of this verse differ: some take it to mean the opposite.)",
 "So all of you, holding your positions at every point of entry, must protect Bhishma above all.",
 "Then Bhishma, the mighty grandsire and eldest of the Kurus, roared like a lion and blew his conch, filling Duryodhana with joy.",
 "At once conches and kettledrums, cymbals, drums and horns blared together, and the sound was tremendous.",
 "Then, standing in their great chariot yoked to white horses, Krishna and Arjuna blew their divine conches.",
 "Krishna blew Panchajanya, Arjuna blew Devadatta, and Bhima, doer of fearsome deeds, blew his great conch Paundra.",
 "King Yudhishthira, son of Kunti, blew Anantavijaya; Nakula and Sahadeva blew Sughosha and Manipushpaka.",
 "The king of Kashi, a supreme archer; Shikhandi, the great chariot-warrior; Dhrishtadyumna, Virata and the unconquered Satyaki;",
 "Drupada, the sons of Draupadi and the mighty-armed son of Subhadra each blew their own conch, O lord of the earth.",
 "That roar echoed through earth and sky and tore at the hearts of Dhritarashtra's sons.",
 "Then, seeing Dhritarashtra's sons arrayed for battle and the weapons about to fly, Arjuna, whose banner bore Hanuman, lifted his bow",
 "and spoke to Krishna. Arjuna said: Achyuta, draw my chariot up between the two armies,",
 "so that I can look at those who stand here eager to fight, and see whom I must face in this battle.",
 "Let me see the men gathered here to fight, wanting to please the ill-minded son of Dhritarashtra.",
 "Asked this by Arjuna, Krishna drove the finest of chariots between the two armies,",
 "in front of Bhishma, Drona and all the kings of the earth, and said: Partha, see all these Kurus gathered here.",
 "There Arjuna saw, standing in both armies, fathers and grandfathers, teachers, uncles, brothers, sons, grandsons and friends,",
 "fathers-in-law and well-wishers. Seeing all these kinsmen arrayed before him, Arjuna, son of Kunti,",
 "was overcome by deep compassion and spoke in sorrow. Arjuna said: Krishna, seeing my own people eager to fight,",
 "my limbs give way, my mouth goes dry, my body trembles and my hair stands on end.",
 "The Gandiva slips from my hand and my skin is burning. I cannot stand steady; my mind is reeling.",
 "I see evil omens, Keshava, and I see no good in killing my own kin in battle.",
 "I do not want victory, Krishna, nor a kingdom, nor pleasures. What use is a kingdom to us, Govinda, or pleasure, or even life itself?",
 "Those for whose sake we want a kingdom, enjoyment and pleasure stand here in battle, ready to give up their lives and their wealth:",
 "teachers, fathers, sons and grandfathers, uncles, fathers-in-law, grandsons, brothers-in-law and other kinsmen.",
 "I do not want to kill them, Madhusudana, even if they kill me; not for rule over the three worlds, let alone for this earth.",
 "What joy could we have from killing the sons of Dhritarashtra, Janardana? Only sin would come to us from slaying these aggressors.",
 "So it is not right for us to kill the sons of Dhritarashtra, our own kinsmen. How could we be happy, Madhava, after killing our own people?",
 "Even if they, their minds overcome by greed, see no wrong in destroying a family or in betraying friends,",
 "why should we, who see clearly the evil of destroying a family, not learn to turn away from this sin, Janardana?",
 "When a family is destroyed, its timeless traditions perish; and when tradition perishes, disorder overtakes the whole family.",
 "When disorder prevails, Krishna, the women of the family are led astray, and from that comes the mixing of social orders.",
 "That confusion drags the family and its destroyers down to hell, and their ancestors fall too, deprived of the offerings of food and water.",
 "Through these wrongs of those who destroy families and cause the mixing of orders, the age-old duties of community and family are lost.",
 "We have heard, Janardana, that people whose family traditions are destroyed live in hell for a long time.",
 "Alas, how strange that we are set on committing a great sin, ready to kill our own kin out of greed for the pleasures of a kingdom.",
 "It would be better for me if the sons of Dhritarashtra, weapons in hand, killed me in battle, unarmed and not fighting back.",
 "Having spoken this on the battlefield, Arjuna put aside his bow and arrows and sank down on the seat of the chariot, his mind overwhelmed by grief.",
]
CH2 = [
 "To Arjuna, overwhelmed by pity, his eyes full of tears and his heart sinking, Madhusudana spoke these words.",
 "Arjuna, where has this weakness come from at such a moment? It does not befit a noble person, and it leads neither to heaven nor to honour.",
 "Do not give in to this unmanliness, Partha; it does not suit you. Throw off this petty weakness of heart and stand up, scorcher of foes.",
 "Madhusudana, how can I shoot arrows in battle at Bhishma and Drona? They deserve my reverence, slayer of foes.",
 "It would be better to live in this world on alms than to kill these noble teachers. If I killed them, every pleasure and every gain I enjoyed here would be stained with blood.",
 "We do not even know which is better for us, that we conquer them or that they conquer us. The sons of Dhritarashtra stand before us; if we kill them, we would not want to live.",
 "My nature is overcome by weakness and pity, and my mind is confused about my duty. I ask you: tell me for certain what is best. I am your disciple and I have come to you for refuge. Teach me.",
 "I see nothing that could drive away this grief that is drying up my senses, not even an unrivalled, prosperous kingdom on earth or lordship over the gods.",
 "Having said this to Krishna, Arjuna, the scorcher of foes, told Govinda, \"I will not fight,\" and fell silent.",
 "Then, between the two armies, Krishna, as if smiling, spoke these words to the grieving Arjuna.",
 "You grieve for those who need no grief, and yet you speak words that sound wise. The wise grieve neither for the living nor for the dead.",
 "There was never a time when I did not exist, nor you, nor these kings; and there will never be a time when any of us ceases to be.",
 "Just as the soul passes through childhood, youth and old age in this body, so it passes into another body. The steady are not confused by this.",
 "Contact of the senses with their objects brings cold and heat, pleasure and pain. They come and go and do not last. Bear them patiently, Arjuna.",
 "The person these do not disturb, who stays the same in pleasure and pain, steady and wise, is fit for immortality.",
 "The unreal never comes to be, and the real never stops being. Those who see the truth have understood both.",
 "Know that what pervades this whole body is indestructible. No one can destroy what is imperishable.",
 "These bodies of the eternal, indestructible and immeasurable soul are said to come to an end. Therefore fight, Arjuna.",
 "One who thinks the soul is a killer, and one who thinks it is killed, both fail to understand. It neither kills nor is killed.",
 "The soul is never born and never dies. It did not come into being and will not stop being. Unborn, eternal, everlasting and ancient, it is not killed when the body is killed.",
 "Partha, how could one who knows the soul to be indestructible, eternal, unborn and unchanging kill anyone, or make anyone kill?",
 "Just as a person throws away worn-out clothes and puts on new ones, the soul throws away worn-out bodies and enters new ones.",
 "Weapons cannot cut it, fire cannot burn it, water cannot wet it and wind cannot dry it.",
 "It cannot be cut, burned, wetted or dried. It is eternal, present everywhere, stable, unmoving and timeless.",
 "It is called unmanifest, beyond thought and unchanging. Knowing it to be so, you should not grieve.",
 "And even if you think the soul is constantly born and constantly dies, mighty-armed one, you still have no reason to grieve.",
 "Death is certain for whoever is born, and birth is certain for whoever dies. So you should not grieve over what cannot be avoided.",
 "Beings are unmanifest before birth, manifest in the middle, and unmanifest again at death. What is there to mourn, Arjuna?",
 "Some see the soul as a wonder, some speak of it as a wonder, and some hear of it as a wonder; yet even after hearing, no one truly knows it.",
 "The soul that lives in every body can never be killed, Arjuna. So you should not grieve for any being.",
 "Considering your own duty as well, you should not waver. For a warrior there is nothing better than a war fought for what is right.",
 "Fortunate are the warriors, Partha, to whom such a war comes unsought, like an open door to heaven.",
 "But if you refuse to fight this righteous war, you will give up your duty and your honour, and you will incur sin.",
 "People will tell of your disgrace for ever, and for someone who has been honoured, disgrace is worse than death.",
 "The great warriors will think you fled the battle out of fear, and those who held you in high regard will think little of you.",
 "Your enemies will say many unspeakable things, mocking your strength. What could be more painful than that?",
 "If you are killed, you will reach heaven; if you win, you will enjoy the earth. So stand up, son of Kunti, determined to fight.",
 "Treating pleasure and pain, gain and loss, victory and defeat alike, get ready for battle. That way you will incur no sin.",
 "I have explained this wisdom to you in terms of Sankhya; now hear it in terms of yoga. United with this understanding, Partha, you will break the bonds of karma.",
 "On this path no effort is ever wasted and no harm is done. Even a little of this practice protects you from great fear.",
 "Here the determined mind is focused on one thing, Arjuna; the thoughts of the undetermined are endless and branch in every direction.",
 "Those without discernment speak in flowery words, delighting in the letter of the Vedas and saying there is nothing more, Partha.",
 "Full of desires and intent on heaven, they recommend many elaborate rites aimed at pleasure and power, which only bring rebirth as the fruit of action.",
 "For those attached to pleasure and power, whose minds are carried away by such talk, the determined, focused mind does not arise in meditation.",
 "The Vedas deal with the three qualities of nature. Rise above them, Arjuna: free from the pairs of opposites, always grounded in truth, unconcerned with getting and keeping, and centred in the Self.",
 "To someone who knows Brahman, all the Vedas are as useful as a small pond in a land flooded with water on every side.",
 "You have a right to your work, but never to its fruits. Do not let the fruits of action be your motive, and do not cling to inaction either.",
 "Do your work grounded in yoga, Dhananjaya, letting go of attachment and staying even-minded in success and failure. This evenness of mind is called yoga.",
 "Work done for its rewards is far below work guided by wisdom, Dhananjaya. Take refuge in that wisdom; those who work only for the fruits are to be pitied.",
 "One united with this wisdom leaves behind both good and bad deeds even in this life. So commit yourself to yoga: yoga is skill in action.",
 "The wise, united with this understanding, let go of the fruits of action, are freed from the bonds of birth, and reach the state beyond all sorrow.",
 "When your understanding crosses the swamp of delusion, you will no longer care about what you have heard or what is still to be heard.",
 "When your understanding, unsettled by conflicting teachings, stands firm and unmoving in meditation, you will reach yoga.",
 "Keshava, how would you describe someone whose wisdom is steady and who is absorbed in meditation? How does such a person speak, sit and walk?",
 "When a person lets go of every desire of the mind, Partha, and is content in the Self through the Self, they are said to have steady wisdom.",
 "One who is not shaken by sorrow, does not long for pleasure, and is free from attachment, fear and anger is called a sage of steady mind.",
 "One who is unattached everywhere, who neither celebrates good fortune nor resents bad, has wisdom that is firmly established.",
 "When, like a tortoise drawing in its limbs, a person withdraws the senses from their objects, their wisdom is firmly established.",
 "Sense objects fall away from one who abstains from them, but the taste for them remains; even that taste disappears once the Supreme is seen.",
 "The restless senses, son of Kunti, can forcibly carry away the mind even of a wise person who is striving for control.",
 "Restraining them all, one should sit in yoga, devoted to Me. Whoever has their senses under control has steady wisdom.",
 "When a person dwells on sense objects, attachment to them grows; from attachment comes desire, and from desire comes anger.",
 "From anger comes delusion; from delusion, a confused memory; from a confused memory, the loss of reason; and when reason is lost, the person is ruined.",
 "But a disciplined person who moves among sense objects with senses under control, free from attraction and aversion, reaches inner calm.",
 "In that calm all sorrows come to an end, and the understanding of the calm-hearted quickly becomes steady.",
 "One who is not disciplined has no wisdom and no focus; without focus there is no peace, and without peace, how can there be happiness?",
 "Just as a strong wind sweeps a boat off course on the water, a mind that follows the wandering senses carries away one's wisdom.",
 "So, mighty-armed one, the person whose senses are fully withdrawn from their objects has wisdom that is firmly established.",
 "What is night for all beings is wakefulness for the self-disciplined; and what is wakefulness for all beings is night for the sage who truly sees.",
 "Just as the ocean stays full and still while rivers keep flowing into it, the person into whom desires flow in the same way finds peace, not the one who chases desires.",
 "Whoever lets go of all desires and lives free from longing, without the sense of \"mine\" and without ego, finds peace.",
 "This is the state of Brahman, Partha. Having reached it, one is no longer deluded; established in it even at the time of death, one attains the peace of Brahman.",
]
assert len(CH1) == 47 and len(CH2) == 72, (len(CH1), len(CH2))
